11. Limitations to mention honestly
This is very important in interviews. Showing that you understand production tradeoffs makes your answer stronger.
“For a local demo and single-server project, JSON storage is simple and effective. However, it is not suitable for a production service with many users or multiple servers.”
Production improvements:
Replace JSON storage with PostgreSQL or MongoDB.
Add login and user accounts.
Add ownership so one user cannot view or delete another user’s links.
Use Redis for caching and rate limiting.
Add HTTPS and a custom domain.
Add protection against malicious or phishing links.
Store detailed click events asynchronously for country, device, and referrer analytics.
Add automated tests, logging, monitoring, and backups.
Deploy using Docker, a cloud provider, and a reverse proxy/load balancer.
12. Strong 60-second interview answer
“I built Shortly, a full-stack URL shortener application. A user submits a long URL through a responsive dashboard, and the Node.js backend validates it, generates or accepts a custom alias, and stores the record persistently. When someone visits the short URL, the server finds the matching code, updates its click analytics, and sends a 302 redirect to the original destination.  
I implemented REST APIs for creating, listing, and deleting links, and used clean separation between frontend, backend, and storage responsibilities. For the demo I used JSON persistence to avoid unnecessary dependencies, but for production I would replace it with PostgreSQL, add authentication, Redis-based rate limiting, async analytics, HTTPS, and abuse protection.”

